{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem remembered Sarah as He had said, and HaShem did for Sarah as He had spoken.</b> There is a difference between the expression פְּקִידָה (remembering) and the expression עֲשִׂיָּה (doing), for פְּקִידָה is nothing but a general remembrance, while עֲשִׂיָּה is actual action, as it is written: ״וְאֶעֶשְׂךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״ (Genesis 12:2). And it is concluded in Bereishit Rabbah (39:11) that the expression דִּבּוּר (speaking) indicates more affection than the expression אֲמִירָה (saying), because דִּבּוּר indicates that the matters were articulated explicitly with the precision of the tongue, and this relates to the expression of עֲשִׂיָּה." "And the explanation of the matter is that when Sarah was remembered, all the barren women were remembered with her, as I explained in the portion of לֶךְ לְךָ on the verse ״וְהָיְתָה לְגוֹיִם״ (Genesis 17:16). And even though they were remembered with her, nevertheless they were not equal to Sarah, for Sarah's remembering was in the language of actual doing, through the action of the hands of HaShem, blessed be He, as it is written (Midrash Tehillim 139:5): ״גְּדוֹלִים צַדִּיקִים מִמַּעֲשֵׂה שָׁמַיִם וָאָרֶץ, כִּי הַצַּדִּיקִים נִבְרְאוּ בִּשְׁתֵּי יָדַיִם״ etc. — that is, actual doing — and in the language of דִּבּוּר, which indicates affection, as it is written (Genesis 15:4): ״וְהִנֵּה דְבַר ה׳ אֵלָיו לֹא יִירָשְׁךָ זֶה, כִּי אִם אֲשֶׁר יֵצֵא מִמֵּעֶיךָ״ etc. But the other barren women were in the language of פְּקִידָה alone, and in the language of אֲמִירָה, which does not contain so much affection — for the portion of לֶךְ לְךָ begins in the language of אֲמִירָה, and there it is stated (Genesis 17:16): ״וּבֵרַכְתִּי אֹתָהּ וְהָיְתָה לְגוֹיִם״ — meaning that she would be a help to all the nations, for the barren women of the nations of the lands would be remembered with her. This is what is written: ״וַה׳ פָּקַד אֶת שָׂרָה״ — as though it said 'with Sarah,' for the word ״אֶת״ serves as the expression of 'with,' as in ״הַבָּאִים מִצְרַיְמָה אֵת יַעֲקֹב״ (Exodus 1:1). Those barren women who were remembered with Sarah were remembered only in the language of פְּקִידָה and in the language of אֲמִירָה. However, ״וַיַּעַשׂ ה׳ לְשָׂרָה״ — meaning for Sarah herself — in the language of דִּבּוּר, ״כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֵּר״, and actual doing. By way of parable: someone who does not greatly love his fellow, even though he sometimes remembers him, nevertheless does not do so much on his account; but for one whom he loves, he enters even through the thickness of a beam and acts on his behalf. And as for what is said, ״וַה׳״ — with a vav — our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Bereishit Rabbah 53:2): ״Every place where ׳וַה׳׳ is said, it means He and His court of justice.״ A support from here for the words of our Rabbis, of blessed memory (Rosh HaShanah 11a), that this פְּקִידָה took place on Rosh HaShanah, on which the Holy One, blessed be He, sits in judgment with all His heavenly court; therefore we read this portion on Rosh HaShanah."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "The phrase ״בְּחֲתִיכַת הַלָּשׁוֹן״ literally 'in the cutting of the tongue' is rendered as 'with the precision of the tongue' following the idiom of precise articulation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:07.722553783Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}